  "query_preview": "..\nTonight: Wind will decline and skies will be variably cloudy, \nclearing overnight into early Sunday, as a cold front drives \neastward and drier air slides in. Winds will decline to around \n10-15 mph with gusts of 15-25 mph strongest over the upper \nColumbia Basin to Moses Lake area, declining after 7 to 9 PM. \nWith the decreasing cloud cover and winds and drier air \ntemperatures tonight drop to the lower to mid-30s across much of\nthe region, with mid to upper 20s. This means frost or freezing\ntemperatures are possible.\n\nSunday to Wednesday: Weak high pressure builds in Sunday before\nthe next low approaches early next week. Dry, mostly sunny to \npartly cloudy skies are forecast Sunday. That next low starts to\ndig along the coast Sunday night into Monday, stretches and \ntracks largely toward the California coast before slowly moving \ninland there toward midweek. This keeps the brunt of the \nprecipitation potential south of our areas, but look for \nincreasing clouds for Sunday night into Tuesday. A boundary \nstalled near the southeast CWA could be a focal point for some \nprecipitation, but the probability for measurable precipition is\ngenerally less than 15% per the NBM and LREF. Some breezy \nnortherly winds are forecast down the Okanogan Valley to \nWaterville Plateau on Tuesday in the tightening north-south \ngradient, with gusts of 15-25 mph. Otherwise look for highs in \nthe lower to mid-60s and lows largely in the 30s, with some \nupper 20s in the sheltered northeast valleys. Those highs are\nabout 4-8 degrees above normal.\n\nThursday to Saturday: Another trough approaches from the \nnorthwest Thursday and swings a system through into Friday.\nMoisture being drawn up from the south will allows some\nprecipitation chances to emerge over southeast WA/lower ID\nThursday and a broader portion of the area Thursday night into\nFriday. However, central WA is apt to remain dry. Other shower\nchances linger Saturday. Over precipitation amounts remain on\nthe lighter side. The main potential for >=0.10\" over the\n48-hour period from Thursday morning through Friday night is\nover the Cascade Crest and the lower and central ID Panhandle,\nwhere probabilities are around 40 to 60%. Snow levels and\ntemperature do trend down, but ensemble box-and-whisker plots\nshow a range of about 10-15 degrees between the 25th and 75th\npercentile for temperature and about a 2000 foot difference in\nsnow levels. It will be mainly mountain snow and valley rain,\nwith the best chance for any snow dropping to pass level late\nFriday into Saturday. However given the spread, confidences are\nlow and right now amounts look light if anything. Highs right\nnow are forecast to be in the 60s Thursday, dropping into 50s\nFriday and next Saturday; lows are forecast to be in the 30s to\nlower 40s, dropping toward the 20s and 30s Friday night into\nnext Saturday AM. Some breezy conditions come with the Thursday\nnight and Friday pattern chances, right now gusting 15-20 mph\nlocally near 25-30 mph over the Upper Columbia Basin/Waterville\nPlateau Friday afternoon. It is several days out so confidence\nin all these details is low to medium; but broadly speaking it\nlooks cooler with increased precipitation chances by the end of\nthe work week. /Solveig",
